PHASE 4 - SEARCHABLE MEETING MEMORY
===================================
Written 2026-08-21. Implemented and verified the same day. Phases 0 to 3 are
unchanged. The suite is 203 tests, up from 165: 18 for the index, 15 for recall
formatting and the recall prompt, and 5 for the new assistant commands.


What is new
-----------
Retained conversations are now searchable, and a question can be answered from
them with references back to where the answer came from. Three commands:

  SEARCH  <words>       full text search over every retained conversation
  RECALL  <question>    answer the question from a few retrieved passages, cited
  REINDEX               rebuild the index from the canonical transcripts

They take free text, so they are socket/rvwctl commands rather than hotkeys, the
same way the spec's learning #6 said to keep new actions as dispatcher commands.
util/rebuild_index.sh does the same rebuild as REINDEX without a running daemon,
for a cron job or after copying an archive between Macs.


The index is derived data, and stays that way
----------------------------------------------
The transcripts are the record; the index is SQLite FTS5 over them and nothing
more. It lives at var/index/meetings.db (RVW_INDEX_DB moves it), can be deleted
at any moment, and rebuilds from the transcript.jsonl files alone. Principle 7
forbids ever synchronising it between Macs: the transcripts are synchronised and
each Mac rebuilds its own index, which is what REINDEX and rebuild_index.sh are.

SQLite FTS5 ships with Python, ranks by BM25 and needs no new dependency. This is
also why "do not make SQLite the canonical archive" is not contradicted: nothing
canonical is in it. Delete meetings.db and nothing is lost.

One row is one utterance. The words are indexed; everything a hit needs to point
back at its conversation is stored beside them, UNINDEXED so FTS never tokenises
it: the meeting, its date, the utterance's own timestamp, the speaker, and the
directory that also holds the screenshots taken around it. A rebuild reads each
transcript through meeting_archive.read_transcript_records, the same reader the
Markdown rendering uses, so the index cannot parse a line differently from the
rest of the system, and a line that cannot be read back stops the rebuild rather
than being silently skipped.


Only authorised transcripts
---------------------------
"Authorised" is not a new switch to forget to set. An ephemeral session wrote no
transcript, so there is nothing of it to index; only a conversation deliberately
kept is there to be found. A meeting kept but not for searching can still opt out
with "index": false in its metadata.json, which the indexer honours, for the one
kept conversation that should not resurface in a later search.


Search, and what a hit carries
------------------------------
The query is arbitrary user text, so it is turned into a safe FTS MATCH string:
each word is quoted, so punctuation is searched for rather than read as FTS query
syntax and surfaced as an error, and the words are joined with OR. OR is recall;
BM25 is precision. A passage need not contain every word to be a hit, but one
that matches more of them ranks above one that matches fewer, so a stray word in
the query loses nothing and the best passage still comes first.

A hit links back to the meeting, its date, the passage's own timestamp, the
verbatim words, and the screenshots captured within two minutes of the passage in
the same meeting. That last association is by time within the one directory, the
same principle Phase 3 used to associate an image with the speech around it; no
new index of screenshots exists to be lost.


Retrieval augmented recall
--------------------------
RECALL retrieves a few passages -- recall_passage_count, six by default, never
the whole archive -- and asks the local model to answer from those and nothing
else. The passages are numbered, the model is told to cite the numbers it used,
and the same numbered list is printed to me as the answer's sources, each naming
the meeting and pointing at its directory, so an answer that says "[2]" can be
traced to the second source shown. The system prompt holds the model to the
passages and tells it to say when they do not answer the question, because a
confident answer with no source is the thing this phase exists to avoid.

The answer streams to the assistant terminal on the one answering path every
model-facing action already shares; RECALL is a retrieval step, a prompt and a
dispatcher entry, not new answering machinery.


Design decisions
----------------
The index is built once, lazily, on the first SEARCH or RECALL, and REINDEX
refreshes it. Building at start-up would delay every launch for a feature not
every session uses; never building would make the first search surprising. A live
retained session appends to today's transcript while the index is a rebuild
behind, which REINDEX or rebuild_index.sh closes; the MVA does not index each
utterance as it is spoken.

Every index operation happens on the control thread, which serves one command at
a time, so a search and a rebuild never overlap and the index needs no lock of its
own. Each call opens and closes its own SQLite connection, so no connection is
shared between threads.

Semantic/vector retrieval is deliberately not built. The spec asks for it only if
it provides material value, and BM25 over one person's own meetings, where the
words searched for are usually the words that were said, has not yet shown a gap
it would fill. Retrieval sits behind one function, so it can be added later
without touching the commands or the prompt.


Verified
--------
- rebuild_index.sh over a synthetic archive of two meetings reported "indexed 4
  utterance(s) from 2 meeting(s)" and exited 0.
- SEARCH returned the matching passages ranked, each naming its meeting and date
  and time, with the screenshot taken beside the passage associated to it.
- RECALL retrieved the passages, printed the numbered sources pointing at the
  meeting directory, and built a system+user prompt that carries the passages and
  the question and demands citations.
- Deleting meetings.db and rebuilding gave byte-identical search answers, which is
  the property that makes the index disposable.
- An ephemeral meeting contributed nothing; a meeting marked "index": false was
  excluded; a transcript line that is not JSON stopped the rebuild naming the file.
- The full suite is 203 tests, all passing.


